# cgroup v2 migration runbook (target Kubernetes 1.35)

> Generated by cgroupguard. **Nothing in this document has been executed.** Read every step, run commands yourself or through your reviewed pipeline, and stop at the first unexpected result.

## Ground rules

- Work in an agreed maintenance window with an on-call owner; change one group, verify, soak, then continue.
- Every step marked **approval required** must be approved by a named person before it runs.
- Never bypass PodDisruptionBudgets (no `--force`, no `--disable-eviction`). Fix the cause of a blocked drain instead.
- Each disruptive step lists verification and rollback. If verification fails, roll back before continuing.
- Commands use variables (`$CLUSTER_NAME`, `$LOCATION`, `$RESOURCE_GROUP`) that you must set; values that could not be measured are written as `REPLACE_WITH_*` placeholders.

## Assumptions

- 1 Linux node(s) have an unknown cgroup mode. They are not migrated by this plan until the mode is established (step P1.2).

## Migration order

| # | Provider | Pool | Nodes | Pods affected | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | aks | userpool1 | 1 | 2 | canary |
| 2 | eks | workers-al2 | 1 | 2 |  |
| 3 | self-managed |  | 2 | 2 |  |
| 4 | gke | pool-legacy | 2 | 4 |  |

## P1. Prepare and assess

### P1.1 — Set the variables the commands in this plan use

kind: manual · disruption: none

Commands reference shell variables instead of guessed values. Set them yourself: CLUSTER_NAME for managed clusters, LOCATION (GKE region or zone), RESOURCE_GROUP (AKS), and your kubectl context. Nothing in this plan reads or changes them.

### P1.2 — Establish the cgroup mode of nodes that were not measured

kind: inspect · disruption: none

Unknown nodes: mystery-1. On each node run the command below, or re-run the scan with --prometheus-url, --kubelet-metrics (see docs/optional-permissions.md) or the guarded --probe.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup/   # cgroup2fs = v2, tmpfs = v1
```

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cgroups/

### P1.3 — Review disruption protection before touching any node

kind: inspect · disruption: none

Draining respects PodDisruptionBudgets; a workload with one replica, no budget or persistent state is the most exposed. Fragile workloads among the findings (single replica, no budget, or persistent state): batch/Job/reader, obs/DaemonSet/metrics, shop/StatefulSet/db.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl get pdb --all-namespaces
kubectl get nodes -o wide
```

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### P1.4 — Take a restorable backup of everything this plan can change

kind: manual · disruption: none

Export or snapshot infrastructure-as-code state, node pool definitions, kubelet and container runtime configuration, and cluster state (for example etcd) in the way your platform documents. Keep the backup until the post-migration soak period ends.

### P1.5 — Agree the migration order and a canary

kind: manual · disruption: none

Groups are ordered by measured blast radius, smallest first. The first group is the canary: complete and verify it, and let it soak, before starting the next.

## P2. Fix workloads that cgroup v2 can break

### W.CG101 — Update JVM images to a build with cgroup v2 container support

kind: manual · disruption: rolling · **approval required** · scope: 1 container(s)

Image tag suggests a version below the documented cgroup v2 support threshold 11.0.16; verify the actual binary and vendor backports. Resources (namespace/kind/name/containerType/container): shop/Deployment/api/regular/app

**Preconditions**

- Change the manifests in your source repository (GitOps) rather than editing live objects.

**Verify**

- kubectl rollout status <kind>/<name> -n <namespace>
- Re-run cgroupguard check; the finding for this rule should be gone or reduced to a note.

**Rollback**

- Revert the manifest change in source control and let your delivery pipeline roll back, or run kubectl rollout undo <kind>/<name> -n <namespace>.

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cgroups/

