## P3. Migrate node groups to cgroup v2 (one group at a time, canary first)

### M1.1 — Add a replacement node pool on a cgroup v2 OS image

kind: command · disruption: platform · **approval required** · scope: aks pool userpool1, 1 nodes, 2 pods

CANARY GROUP (smallest measured blast radius). Ubuntu 22.04 is listed as cgroup v2 on the AKS supported-versions page. If the old pool is the cluster's only system pool, create the replacement with --mode System first: a system pool can only be deleted if another system pool exists. Labels, taints, zones, max pods and autoscaling settings are not copied; add the matching flags from the old pool definition. Linux pool names are limited to 12 characters.

**Preconditions**

- Backup from P1.4 is complete.
- Regional quota exists for the extra nodes.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
az aks nodepool add --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --cluster-name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --name userpool1v2 --os-type Linux --os-sku Ubuntu2204 --mode User --node-count 1 --node-vm-size Standard_D4s_v3
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes -L kubernetes.azure.com/agentpool
- cgroupguard check --target-version 1.35 --kubelet-metrics   # new nodes should report v2

**Rollback**

- Delete only the new pool: az aks nodepool delete --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --cluster-name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --name userpool1v2

Sources: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/upgrade-os-version, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/create-node-pools

### M1.2 — Cordon the old nodes

kind: command · disruption: node-drain · **approval required** · scope: old pool userpool1

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl cordon aks-c-1
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes   # old nodes show SchedulingDisabled

**Rollback**

- kubectl uncordon aks-c-1

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### M1.3 — Drain the old nodes one at a time

kind: command · disruption: node-drain · **approval required** · scope: old pool userpool1

Draining uses the eviction API and respects PodDisruptionBudgets. Do not use --disable-eviction or --force. If a drain blocks on a budget, fix the cause instead of bypassing it.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl drain aks-c-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=30m
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o wide --field-selector spec.nodeName=aks-c-1
- Confirm replacement pods are Ready on the new nodes before the next drain.

**Rollback**

- kubectl uncordon aks-c-1   # pods are rescheduled by their controllers

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### M1.4 — Delete the old node pool after a soak period

kind: command · disruption: node-replace · **approval required** · scope: old pool userpool1

Irreversible. Microsoft documents: "When you delete a node pool, AKS doesn't perform cordon and drain", so the previous steps are mandatory.

**Preconditions**

- Workloads run healthy on the new pool.
- The old pool definition is saved in source control or backed up.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
az aks nodepool delete --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --cluster-name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --name userpool1
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes -L kubernetes.azure.com/agentpool

**Rollback**

- Deletion cannot be undone. Recreate the pool from the saved definition and re-run the migration steps.

Sources: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/resize-node-pool

### M2.1 — Review the replacement managed node group

kind: config · disruption: none · scope: eks pool workers-al2, 1 nodes, 2 pods

AL2 is cgroup v1; AWS recommends AL2023 or Bottlerocket, which enable cgroup v2 by default. For managed node groups without a custom-AMI launch template AWS requires a blue/green migration: create a new group with the new AMI type, move workloads, delete the old group. Replace the REPLACE_WITH_* placeholders (they are not known from the cluster). For Bottlerocket use amiFamily: Bottlerocket instead.

**File `eks/workers-al2-nodegroup.yaml`** (written to the output directory when `--output-dir` is used)

```
# Review before use. Sizes are copied from the current node count; set min/max to match the old group.
apiVersion: eksctl.io/v1alpha5
kind: ClusterConfig
metadata:
  name: REPLACE_WITH_CLUSTER_NAME
  region: REPLACE_WITH_REGION
managedNodeGroups:
  - name: workers-al2-al2023
    amiFamily: AmazonLinux2023
    instanceType: m5.large
    desiredCapacity: 1
    minSize: 1
    maxSize: 2
```

Sources: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/al2023.html, https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-ami-deprecation-faqs.html, https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/eksctl/custom-ami-support.html

### M2.2 — Create the replacement node group

kind: command · disruption: platform · **approval required** · scope: eks pool workers-al2, 1 nodes, 2 pods

Creates new nodes next to the old ones; nothing is drained yet. AL2023 needs eksctl 0.176.0 or newer and VPC CNI 1.16.2 or newer; nodeadm replaces bootstrap.sh for custom launch templates.

**Preconditions**

- Placeholders in eks/workers-al2-nodegroup.yaml are replaced and the file is reviewed.
- CoreDNS has at least 2 replicas and any cluster autoscaler is paused for the move.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
eksctl create nodegroup --config-file=eks/workers-al2-nodegroup.yaml
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes -L eks.amazonaws.com/nodegroup
- cgroupguard check --target-version 1.35 --kubelet-metrics   # new nodes should report v2

**Rollback**

- Delete only the new node group: eksctl delete nodegroup --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --name workers-al2-al2023

Sources: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/al2023.html, https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/migrate-stack.html

### M2.3 — Cordon the old nodes

kind: command · disruption: node-drain · **approval required** · scope: old node group workers-al2

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl cordon eks-b-1
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes   # old nodes show SchedulingDisabled

**Rollback**

- kubectl uncordon eks-b-1

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### M2.4 — Drain the old nodes one at a time

kind: command · disruption: node-drain · **approval required** · scope: old node group workers-al2

Draining uses the eviction API and respects PodDisruptionBudgets. Do not use --disable-eviction or --force. If a drain blocks on a budget, fix the cause instead of bypassing it.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl drain eks-b-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=30m
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o wide --field-selector spec.nodeName=eks-b-1
- Confirm replacement pods are Ready on the new nodes before the next drain.

**Rollback**

- kubectl uncordon eks-b-1   # pods are rescheduled by their controllers

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### M2.5 — Delete the old node group after a soak period

kind: command · disruption: node-replace · **approval required** · scope: old node group workers-al2

Irreversible. Keep the old group, drained and cordoned, through an agreed soak period before deleting it.

**Preconditions**

- Workloads run healthy on the new group.
- The old group's definition is saved in source control or backed up.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
eksctl delete nodegroup --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --name workers-al2
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get nodes -L eks.amazonaws.com/nodegroup

**Rollback**

- Deletion cannot be undone. Recreate the group from the saved definition and re-run the migration steps.

Sources: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/migrate-stack.html

### M3.1 — Check that each node can run cgroup v2

kind: inspect · disruption: none · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

Requirements: Linux kernel 5.8 or newer, containerd 1.4+ or cri-o 1.20+, and the systemd cgroup driver in both the kubelet and the runtime. Nodes, in the order they should be changed (one at a time): onprem-1, onprem-2.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
uname -r
containerd --version   # or: crio --version
stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup/   # tmpfs = v1, cgroup2fs = v2
```

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cgroups/, https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes/

### M3.2 — Cordon and drain one node

kind: command · disruption: node-drain · **approval required** · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

Repeat steps M3.2 to M3.6 for each node in the order above, finishing and verifying one node before the next. Example node: onprem-1.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl cordon onprem-1
kubectl drain onprem-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=30m
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get node onprem-1
- Replacement pods are Ready elsewhere.

**Rollback**

- kubectl uncordon onprem-1

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/

### M3.3 — Back up the node's boot, runtime and kubelet configuration

kind: command · disruption: config-change · **approval required** · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
sudo cp -a /etc/default/grub /etc/default/grub.cgroupguard.bak
sudo cp -a /etc/containerd/config.toml /etc/containerd/config.toml.cgroupguard.bak
sudo cp -a /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml.cgroupguard.bak
```

**Verify**

- ls -l /etc/default/grub.cgroupguard.bak /etc/containerd/config.toml.cgroupguard.bak /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml.cgroupguard.bak

**Rollback**

- Nothing to undo: these are copies. Paths vary by distribution and installer; adjust them to your node.

### M3.4 — Enable cgroup v2 and the systemd cgroup driver on the node

kind: config · disruption: config-change · **approval required** · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

Debian/Ubuntu: add systemd.unified_cgroup_hierarchy=1 to GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in /etc/default/grub, then run update-grub (RHEL-like systems: use grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args="systemd.unified_cgroup_hierarchy=1"). In containerd set SystemdCgroup = true under [plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]; in the kubelet configuration set cgroupDriver: systemd. The kubelet and the runtime must use the same cgroup driver.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
sudo sed -i 's/^GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="\(.*\)"/GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="\1 systemd.unified_cgroup_hierarchy=1"/' /etc/default/grub
sudo update-grub
# set SystemdCgroup = true in /etc/containerd/config.toml and cgroupDriver: systemd in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
```

**Verify**

- grep unified_cgroup_hierarchy /etc/default/grub
- grep -n SystemdCgroup /etc/containerd/config.toml
- grep -n cgroupDriver /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml

**Rollback**

- Restore the *.cgroupguard.bak copies and run update-grub (or grubby) again before rebooting.

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cgroups/, https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes/

### M3.5 — Reboot the node and confirm cgroup v2

kind: command · disruption: node-reboot · **approval required** · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
sudo systemctl reboot
# after it returns:
stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup/
```

**Verify**

- stat prints cgroup2fs
- kubectl get node onprem-1   # Ready

**Rollback**

- Boot the previous kernel command line from the GRUB menu, or restore the backups from step M3.3, then reboot. Do not uncordon a node that is not Ready.

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cgroups/

### M3.6 — Uncordon the node and let it soak

kind: command · disruption: none · scope: self-managed, 2 nodes, 2 pods

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl uncordon onprem-1
```

**Verify**

- cgroupguard check --target-version <target> --kubelet-metrics   # the node reports v2

### M4.1 — Review the node pool and its upgrade strategy

kind: inspect · disruption: none · scope: gke pool pool-legacy, 2 nodes, 4 pods

GKE applies a cgroup mode change by re-creating nodes with its surge upgrade strategy, immediately and without respecting maintenance policies. Choose max-surge or blue-green settings first if the default is too aggressive. If you prefer a blue/green replacement, generate the module with --emit terraform (terraform/gke-<pool>/). Autopilot clusters and auto-provisioned pools use a cluster-level command instead: gcloud container clusters update "$CLUSTER_NAME" --autoprovisioning-cgroup-mode=v2.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
gcloud container node-pools describe pool-legacy --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --location "$LOCATION"
# optional: gcloud container node-pools update pool-legacy --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --location "$LOCATION" --max-surge-upgrade=1 --max-unavailable-upgrade=0
```

Sources: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/migrate-cgroupv2, https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/node-pool-upgrade-strategies

### M4.2 — Review the node system configuration file

kind: config · disruption: none · scope: gke pool pool-legacy, 2 nodes, 4 pods

Save this file and review it. It sets only the cgroup mode.

**File `gke/pool-legacy-system-config.yaml`** (written to the output directory when `--output-dir` is used)

```
linuxConfig:
  cgroupMode: 'CGROUP_MODE_V2'
```

Sources: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/node-system-config

### M4.3 — Apply cgroup v2 to the node pool (nodes are re-created)

kind: command · disruption: node-replace · **approval required** · scope: gke pool pool-legacy, 2 nodes, 4 pods

Documented by Google: "This change requires re-creating the nodes, which can cause disruption to your running workloads." Run during an agreed window; protect workloads with PodDisruptionBudgets first.

**Preconditions**

- Backup from P1.4 is complete.
- PodDisruptionBudgets reviewed in P1.3.
- The pool runs a GKE version that supports the cgroup mode setting.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
gcloud container node-pools update pool-legacy --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --location "$LOCATION" --system-config-from-file=gke/pool-legacy-system-config.yaml
```

**Verify**

- gcloud container node-pools describe pool-legacy --cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" --location "$LOCATION"
- cgroupguard check --target-version 1.35 --kubelet-metrics   # nodes should report v2

**Rollback**

- Re-apply a system configuration file with cgroupMode: 'CGROUP_MODE_V1' using the same command; this re-creates the nodes again. GKE removes cgroup v1 support with minor version 1.35, so on 1.35 and later rollback to v1 is not available: roll back workload changes instead.
- If nodes fail to become Ready, stop and open a Google Cloud support case; do not delete the pool.

Sources: https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/migrate-cgroupv2, https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/node-system-config

## P4. Stopgap (not recommended): allow the kubelet on cgroup v1

### S.1 — Temporarily set failCgroupV1: false until 2026-12-31

kind: config · disruption: config-change · **approval required**

This only lets the kubelet start on a cgroup v1 node. It does not make workloads or the node compatible, and it is not available on every managed platform. Kubernetes deprecates cgroup v1 and plans removal, so treat 2026-12-31 as a hard deadline owned by a named person. Remove the setting when the node is migrated.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
# In the kubelet configuration file on each affected node, add:
#   failCgroupV1: false
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
```

**File `stopgap/kubelet-failcgroupv1.yaml`** (written to the output directory when `--output-dir` is used)

```
# TEMPORARY. Remove by 2026-12-31.
apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
failCgroupV1: false
```

**Verify**

- kubectl get node <node> -o wide
- systemctl status kubelet

**Rollback**

- Remove failCgroupV1: false (the default is true) and restart the kubelet; the node must be on cgroup v2 first or the kubelet will refuse to start.

Sources: https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/config-api/kubelet-config.v1beta1/

## P5. Verify

### V.1 — Re-scan the cluster

kind: inspect · disruption: none

Expected: every Linux node reports cgroup v2 from direct evidence (metric, Prometheus or probe). A clean scan is still not an upgrade certification.

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
cgroupguard check --target-version 1.35 --exit-error --format json
```

### V.2 — Confirm workload health

kind: inspect · disruption: none

**Commands (review, do not paste blindly)**

```bash
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl get pods --all-namespaces --field-selector=status.phase!=Running,status.phase!=Succeeded
```

## Limitations of the scan behind this plan

- Offline evidence is user-supplied and is not authenticated or checked for freshness.
- Workload analysis uses image tags and allowlisted manifest path references; no image layers, runtime binaries, mounted configs or application behavior are inspected.
- Kubelet configuration, enabled cgroup controllers and other upgrade prerequisites remain unassessed. A clean heuristic scan is not proof of cluster readiness.
- Offline snapshots are user-supplied, unauthenticated evidence with no freshness guarantee.
